---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Glossary from "../components/Glossary.astro";
// @ts-ignore -- plain-JS helper: finds each entry in the chapter text at build time
import { glossaryIndex } from "../lib/glossary.mjs";
// @ts-ignore -- plain-JS data, and deliberately free of imports so Storybook can load it
import { GROUPS, TERMS, ABSENT } from "../lib/greek.mjs";

const { groups, total, absent } = glossaryIndex({ groups: GROUPS, terms: TERMS, absent: ABSENT, label: "greek" });
---

<Layout title="Greek glossary">
  <header class="mb-8">
    <p class="eyebrow">Appendix</p>
    <h1 class="mt-1 font-serif text-3xl font-semibold">Greek glossary</h1>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-prose text-lg text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-400">
      {total} words from the Greek — and, at the end, from the Hebrew and Aramaic — with what they mean and the
      paragraphs where Hobbes uses them. There is more Greek here than
      <a href="/latin/" class="chapter-link">Latin</a>, and it does a different job: where the Latin is mostly
      terms of art, the Greek is almost all etymology, produced to show that a word the church has made into a
      mystery began as an ordinary word for an ordinary thing.
    </p>
  </header>

  <Glossary
    groups={groups}
    absent={absent}
    absentIntro="Two Greek phrases a reader arrives expecting, and one Hebrew name that belongs to a different book."
  />

  <section class="mt-16 max-w-prose">
    <h2 id="on-the-glossary" class="section-heading">
      <a href="#on-the-glossary" class="heading-anchor">On the glossary</a>
    </h2>
    <div class="prose-chapter mt-4 font-serif text-lg leading-relaxed">
      <p>
        The 1651 text has no Greek type in it. Every word is transliterated into roman letters, unaccented, and
        the endings are anglicised as often as not — <em>Pisteno Eis</em> for the printer's version of
        πιστεύω εἰς. So the headwords here keep his spelling, since that is what a reader will meet on the page,
        and the word in its own alphabet beside it is supplied rather than quoted: it is what he was
        transliterating, not what he set. The citations are the other way round, located in the chapter text at
        build time, so they stay in step with it.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Hebrew and Aramaic are folded in at the end rather than given a page. There is not much of it, and
        almost all of it arrives second hand: Hobbes quotes the Septuagint's Greek or Jerome's Latin for the
        Hebrew words he cares about, which is itself part of his argument that no priesthood holds a key the rest
        of us lack.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
